<?xml version="1.0" ?>
<!--
  Mode-aware navigation survey world.

  Paper 2 qualitative demonstration scene. This world is for *seeing* what the
  mode manager does; it contributes no statistics. All campaign numbers come from
  the headless deterministic runner.

  Visual design notes

  Underwater appearance is carried by four things, in order of impact:
    1. exponential-squared fog tinted blue-green, which is what actually reads as
       "underwater" rather than "dark room";
    2. a dim, strongly blue-shifted directional light standing in for surface
       daylight filtered through the water column;
    3. low ambient with the background matched to the fog colour, so distant
       geometry dissolves into the water instead of ending at a visible edge;
    4. warm vehicle lamps, which contrast against the cold ambient and make the
       backscatter story legible on camera.

  The vehicle carries BOTH lamp positions used by the study: a coaxial lamp
  beside the camera and an off-axis lamp on a 0.35 m baseline, so the geometry
  behind the off-axis result is visible in the render, not just in the maths.
-->
<sdf version="1.9">
  <world name="mode_aware_survey">

    <!-- An 8 ms step, not the 1 ms one Gazebo defaults to.

         At 1 ms this world asks for a thousand physics steps a second across a
         hundred models, and the server managed about a fifth of that: a
         measured real-time factor of 0.18 to 0.22 on eight cores, with the
         server process single-thread bound at 140% CPU. Everything downstream
         of the clock inherited that. Sensors declared at 50 Hz arrived at 10,
         the camera declared at 15 Hz arrived at about 3, and the whole scene
         moved and drew at a fifth speed, which reads as sluggish, juddering
         motion however fast the graphics card is.

         Measured on the same machine, same scene:

           1 ms   real-time factor 0.18 to 0.22,  odometry  10 Hz
           4 ms   real-time factor 0.46 to 0.55,  odometry  27 Hz
           8 ms   real-time factor 1.00,          odometry  50 Hz

         8 ms is where it reaches real time, so that is what it runs at.

         Nothing here needs the finer step. The vehicle's link is kinematic and
         its pose is written by the vehicle node, so no integration error can
         reach it. The scenery moves under velocity control at constant speed,
         which is exact at any step size. There are no contacts, no joints and
         no stiff constraints anywhere in the world; the seabed is visual only.
         The dynamics behind every reported number are in mission.py and never
         touch this engine at all. -->
    <physics name="8ms" type="dart">
      <max_step_size>0.008</max_step_size>
      <real_time_factor>1.0</real_time_factor>
    </physics>

    <plugin filename="gz-sim-physics-system"           name="gz::sim::systems::Physics"/>
    <plugin filename="gz-sim-user-commands-system"     name="gz::sim::systems::UserCommands"/>
    <plugin filename="gz-sim-scene-broadcaster-system" name="gz::sim::systems::SceneBroadcaster"/>
    <plugin filename="gz-sim-sensors-system"           name="gz::sim::systems::Sensors">
      <render_engine>ogre2</render_engine>
    </plugin>
    <plugin filename="gz-sim-imu-system"               name="gz::sim::systems::Imu"/>
    <!-- Buoyancy is deliberately NOT enabled.
         The vehicle is posed directly from the simulated state, so its motion is
         prescribed rather than dynamic; that is the fidelity this study needs, since
         the questions are about sensing and decision-making rather than about
         vehicle dynamics. Simulating a buoyant force on top of a commanded
         velocity is contradictory, and the two fight: with the buoyancy system
         active, a sustained +0.3 m/s vertical command produced exactly zero
         vertical motion over twelve seconds while horizontal commands were
         followed to seven decimal places. The altitude action — the strongest
         lever the manager has — was therefore inert in the demonstrator.
         A dynamics-faithful version belongs with a thruster-and-force model,
         not with velocity control. -->

    <!-- ================= scene: the underwater look ================= -->
    <scene>
      <!-- NO <sky>. It is tempting, and it has been tried twice. Gazebo's sky
           is not a backdrop, it is a global light source: switching it on
           floods the scene, washes the seabed to pale haze and destroys every
           trace of the depth the lamps are supposed to reveal. It is also the
           wrong thing physically, because the camera is underwater and what is
           overhead is the underside of the sea, which the surface mesh already
           provides. The bright band above the waterline is the background
           colour, lit to read as daylight seen from below.

           Cold, dim ambient. Deep water is not black, it is desaturated teal. -->
      <!-- Ambient is near-neutral and no longer dim.
           Every light in this scene used to be blue-teal, and the consequence
           was that nothing had a colour of its own: a sand seabed lit only by
           blue light reflects blue, so the bottom, the surface and the water
           all rendered as the same flat wash. That is not what water does.
           Water tints what passes THROUGH it, over distance; it does not
           repaint every surface at zero range.

           So the light is roughly white with a cool bias, and the blue comes
           from the background and from the surface material instead. Near
           surfaces now show sediment as sediment and coral as coral, which is
           also the effect the vehicle lamps exist to produce. -->
      <ambient>0.34 0.36 0.36 1</ambient>
      <!-- Background is doing the work that fog cannot. KNOWN LIMITATION:
           Gazebo's ogre2 render engine does not implement <fog> from SDF, and
           silently ignores the block below. That is why the 3D view has a hard
           horizon where the seabed meets the background instead of dissolving
           into it. The block is kept because it is correct SDF and costs
           nothing if a future release honours it.

           What compensates: a very dark blue-green background so distance reads
           as depth rather than as sky, low ambient, and a seabed tint close to
           the background so the horizon is a soft boundary rather than a line
           between two unrelated colours.

           The underwater appearance that this study actually depends on is not
           here at all. It is applied to the camera frame by the propagation
           model, which is the image the optical estimator reads and the one
           shown docked in this window. The 3D view is an external observer's
           view of a scene, deliberately not degraded. -->
      <background>0.05 0.16 0.22 1</background>
      <shadows>true</shadows>
      <grid>false</grid>
      <origin_visual>false</origin_visual>
      <fog>
        <color>0.03 0.10 0.12 1</color>
        <type>exp2</type>
        <density>0.075</density>
      </fog>
    </scene>

    <!-- Surface daylight, heavily attenuated and blue-shifted by the water. -->
    <light type="directional" name="filtered_daylight">
      <cast_shadows>true</cast_shadows>
      <pose>0 0 12 0 0 0</pose>
      <diffuse>0.72 0.76 0.74 1</diffuse>
      <specular>0.22 0.24 0.24 1</specular>
      <attenuation>
        <range>120</range>
        <constant>0.6</constant>
        <linear>0.02</linear>
        <quadratic>0.001</quadratic>
      </attenuation>
      <direction>0.15 0.2 -0.97</direction>
    </light>

    <!-- Faint downwelling fill so the seabed is not pitch black beyond lamp range. -->
    <light type="point" name="water_column_fill">
      <pose>0 0 -6 0 0 0</pose>
      <diffuse>0.20 0.28 0.30 1</diffuse>
      <specular>0.0 0.0 0.0 1</specular>
      <attenuation>
        <range>60</range>
        <constant>0.5</constant>
        <linear>0.03</linear>
        <quadratic>0.002</quadratic>
      </attenuation>
      <cast_shadows>false</cast_shadows>
    </light>

    <!-- ================= seabed ================= -->
    <!-- Base sediment plane. Sand under blue-green water reads olive, not yellow. -->
    <!-- Seabed as a heightmap rather than a plane. A flat floor is the single
         thing that most stopped this looking like the sea, and it also made the
         study's own subject invisible: this is a paper about terrain-relative
         navigation, and terrain matching over a plane is exactly the case that
         cannot work. With relief present the featureless-plain family and the
         terrain-recoverable family are different places rather than the same
         place with a different label.

         The relief comes from uuv_mode_aware_navigation.seabed, and the vehicle
         node samples that same function for altitude. The rendered surface and
         the one the altimeter reads are therefore identical by construction,
         which they were not when the floor was flat and the altitude was a
         constant subtraction.

         <size> is x-extent, y-extent, and the peak-to-trough span the image was
         normalised against; scripts/make_seabed.py prints all three. -->
    <model name="seabed">
      <static>true</static>
      <pose>0 0 -20 0 0 0</pose>
      <link name="link">
        <collision name="collision">
          <geometry><mesh><uri>model://uuv_mode_aware_navigation/models/meshes/seabed.obj</uri></mesh></geometry>
        </collision>
        <visual name="visual">
          <geometry><mesh><uri>model://uuv_mode_aware_navigation/models/meshes/seabed.obj</uri></mesh></geometry>
          <!-- Textured rather than flat-shaded. Flat colour on a smooth mesh is
               the single thing that most makes ground read as a model: there is
               no grain, so the eye has no scale reference and the surface could
               be any size. The maps tile every 3 m, which is fine grain at
               survey altitude and not an obvious repeat from above. -->
          <material>
            <ambient>0.42 0.38 0.30 1</ambient>
            <diffuse>1.0 0.95 0.82 1</diffuse>
            <specular>0.05 0.05 0.04 1</specular>
            <pbr>
              <metal>
                <albedo_map>model://uuv_mode_aware_navigation/models/textures/sand_albedo.png</albedo_map>
                <normal_map>model://uuv_mode_aware_navigation/models/textures/sand_normal.png</normal_map>
                <roughness_map>model://uuv_mode_aware_navigation/models/textures/sand_roughness.png</roughness_map>
                <metalness>0.0</metalness>
              </metal>
            </pbr>
          </material>
        </visual>
      </link>
    </model>

    <!-- Sediment ripples: thin offset slabs give the seabed relief and texture,
         which is what the optical channels actually key off for feature tracking. -->

    <!-- ================= rock field =================
         Scattered relief gives the optical channels real features to track and
         gives the sonar terrain block genuine bathymetric variation. Without
         relief, terrain-aided fixing has nothing to correlate against, which is
         itself one of the conditions the mode manager must infer. -->

    <!-- A low ridge: the terrain-aided navigation block needs relief to correlate
         against. Featureless seabed is a legitimate failure condition and the
         manager must infer it from measured relief, never from a label. -->

    <!-- ================= acoustic beacon =================
         The non-optical aiding modality. Its fix availability depends on range
         and geometry, which is what turns "hold station and wait for a fix" into
         a real navigational decision rather than a free option. -->
    <!-- Scattered boulders. Ellipsoid-ish scaled spheres rather than boxes:
         a box field reads as crates on a floor, which is the single thing that
         most stopped this scene looking like a seabed. Sizes and tints are
         drawn once from a fixed seed so the scene is the same every run. -->
    <model name="boulder_field">
      <static>true</static>
      <link name="link">
        <visual name="bo0"><pose>-1.24 1.55 -19.65 0 0 3.21</pose>
          <geometry><mesh>
            <uri>model://uuv_mode_aware_navigation/models/meshes/rock_0.obj</uri>
            <scale>0.91 0.92 0.78</scale>
          </mesh></geometry>
          <material><ambient>0.07 0.08 0.07 1</ambient>
            <diffuse>0.46 0.44 0.38 1</diffuse>
            <specular>0.05 0.05 0.05 1</specular>
            <pbr>
              <metal>
                <albedo_map>model://uuv_mode_aware_navigation/models/textures/rock_albedo.png</albedo_map>
                <normal_map>model://uuv_mode_aware_navigation/models/textures/rock_normal.png</normal_map>
                <roughness>0.95</roughness><metalness>0.0</metalness>
              </metal>
            </pbr>
          </material>
        </visual>
        <visual name="bo1"><pose>3.38 7.62 -19.88 0 0 0.26</pose>
