    <!-- Downloaded scenery, all at roll 0.

         Gazebo's glTF loader already converts Y-up to Z-up. Adding the usual
         +90 degree roll on top of that therefore tips every model onto its
         side, which is what kept laying the weed flat: it looked like a
         wrong up-axis and was the opposite, a correction applied twice.
         Verified by placing the same plant at roll 0, +90 and -90 side by
         side and looking: only roll 0 stands up.

         Scales come from each model's true bounding box, walked through the
         glTF node transforms rather than read from raw accessor min/max. -->














































    <!-- Downloaded scenery, all at roll 0.

         Gazebo's glTF loader already converts Y-up to Z-up, so adding the usual
         +90 degree roll tips every model onto its side. Verified by placing one
         plant at roll 0, +90 and -90 together and looking: only roll 0 stands.

         The plant pack was one file holding three specimens with DIFFERENT
         conventions: plant_1 modelled Z-up, plant_2 and plant_3 Y-up. That is
         why some stood and some lay flat whatever single roll was applied. They
         are split into plant_1/2/3.gltf, and plant_1 carries a -90 degree X
         rotation baked into its own file, so all three share one convention.

         Each has its own z offset because their origins differ: plant_1 is
         modelled about its middle, the other two about their base. -->
    <!-- ================= WEED PLACEMENT =================

         Each weed carries a <pose> on its visual. It is doing two jobs, and
         both were measured off the meshes' own vertices rather than guessed.

         Standing them up. The three specimens in the pack do not share an up
         axis. plant_2 and plant_3 are modelled Y-up, which is what glTF says
         and what Gazebo's loader converts to Z-up on its own, so they arrive
         upright. plant_1 had a -90 degree X rotation baked into it in an
         earlier attempt to make it match; that turned out to rotate it the
         wrong way, and it arrived lying flat on the sand. Its visual now
         carries roll = +1.5708, which is the rotation that maps its long axis
         back onto Z. Eight of the twenty-four weeds use plant_1, which is why
         exactly a third of them were flat.

         Standing them ON something. The weeds lean by rotating about their
         link origin, so wherever that origin sits is the holdfast. In all three
         meshes it sat somewhere in the middle of the clump, up to 1.44 m above
         the lowest leaf and over a metre to one side. Leaning then swung the
         lower half of the plant down through the seabed, and the harder the
         current the deeper it went. The visual offset puts the centroid of the
         lowest five per cent of the vertices onto the link origin, so the
         holdfast is the pivot and the leaves sweep above it.

         The models were also hoisted 0.6 to 1.6 m above the sand, which had
         been compensating for exactly that: with the mesh hanging below its own
         origin, floating the origin made the plant look planted. Now that the
         base is the origin, each model's z comes from seabed.depth_at at its
         own x and y, which is the same function the altimeter reads, so the
         plants stand on the floor the vehicle flies over rather than near it.

         Finally each visual is lifted by the depth it would otherwise reach at
         full lean, computed per mesh at the 0.7 rad ceiling in fish_school.py:
         0.10 m, 0.09 m and 0.16 m. Nothing dips below the seabed at any
         current, which is the point: the seabed is bedrock, there is nothing
         under it to see.
    -->

    <model name="weed_0">
      <static>false</static>
      <pose>-7.92 -5.19 -19.86 0 0 0.04</pose>
      <link name="link">
        <gravity>false</gravity>  <!-- see SCENERY PHYSICS above -->
        <inertial><mass>0.8</mass><inertia><ixx>0.02</ixx><iyy>0.02</iyy><izz>0.02</izz>
          <ixy>0</ixy><ixz>0</ixz><iyz>0</iyz></inertia></inertial>
        <visual name="v"><pose>1.088 -0.230 0.100 1.5708 0 0</pose><geometry><mesh>
          <uri>model://uuv_mode_aware_navigation/models/external/underwater_plant_pack/plant_1.gltf</uri>
          <scale>0.2190 0.2190 0.2190</scale></mesh></geometry></visual>
      </link>
      <plugin filename="gz-sim-velocity-control-system"
              name="gz::sim::systems::VelocityControl">
        <topic>/model/weed_0/cmd_vel</topic>
      </plugin>
    </model>
